IMPORT prelude

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) -- baseline encoding of the whole operative Act (ss 3-14)
-- from the seat of a party to a civil proceeding (a claimant, or the person answering the claim) whose case is in, or
-- may be moved to or from, the Supreme Court of New South Wales.
--
-- SEAT: a party to the proceeding (s 3 "party ... includes a person who intervenes"). Every question is asked as that
-- party would ask it. The courts' opinions ("it appears to the Supreme Court", "in the opinion of", "the interests of
-- justice", "is satisfied") are inputs, never decided here. See NOTES.md.
--
-- One regulative rule: the s 6(4)-(5)(b) notice to the Attorneys-General, when the court directs a party to give it,
-- run by EVALTRACE inside an exported question that takes only plain facts. The Act fixes no period: the period is the
-- one the court's direction gives.
--
-- Dates: the prelude's built-in date functions only (DATE_SERIAL), not the daydate library.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website, as collected by the Open
-- Australian Legal Corpus on 10 March 2025 (corpus document dated 14 July 2023); may be out of date. State of New South
-- Wales, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 3, 3A -- does the Act reach my case`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 3(1): "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding." s 3A: "This Act does not apply to the jurisdiction of
-- courts with which Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 of the Commonwealth deals." Both are flat
-- negatives: the answer is no (ruling N2). The questions below presume the Act reaches the case (fork F1).
DECLARE `Does the Act reach my case` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your case is a proceeding (a civil one) to which the Act applies (s 3(1))`
    `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act (s 3(1) "proceeding")`
    `no: the Act does not apply to jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (s 3A)`

GIVEN `the case is criminal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is within the Corporations Act jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Does the Act reach my case`
`ss 3, 3A -- the Act reaches the case` `the case is criminal` `the case is within the Corporations Act jurisdiction` MEANS
    IF `the case is criminal`
    THEN `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act (s 3(1) "proceeding")`
    ELSE IF `the case is within the Corporations Act jurisdiction`
         THEN `no: the Act does not apply to jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (s 3A)`
         ELSE `yes: your case is a proceeding (a civil one) to which the Act applies (s 3(1))`

@export Does the NSW cross-vesting Act apply to my court case at all (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 3, 3A)?
GIVEN `the case is criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your case a criminal proceeding (a prosecution), rather than a civil claim?
      `the case is within the Corporations Act jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your case one of those whose court jurisdiction is dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (civil matters under the Corporations legislation)?
GIVETH A `Does the Act reach my case`
DECIDE `Does the Act apply to my case question` IS
    `ss 3, 3A -- the Act reaches the case` `the case is criminal` `the case is within the Corporations Act jurisdiction`

-- s 3(1): "State matter means a matter-- (a) in which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction otherwise than by reason of a
-- law of the Commonwealth or of another State, or (b) removed to the Supreme Court under section 8." A "means"
-- definition: outside both limbs the answer is no.
GIVEN `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 3 -- a State matter` `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IF
        `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction`
    OR  `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8`

@export Is my matter a "State matter" under the NSW cross-vesting Act (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 3(1))?
GIVEN `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the Supreme Court of NSW have jurisdiction in your matter without relying on a law of the Commonwealth or of another State (for example a claim in negligence or under a NSW Act)?
      `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your matter removed into the NSW Supreme Court from a lower NSW court or a tribunal by an order under s 8?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my matter a State matter question` IF
    `s 3 -- a State matter` `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 4 -- can a court of another State hear my NSW matter`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 4(3): "The Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate
-- jurisdiction with respect to State matters." (4): the same for "The State Family Court of another State". (5):
-- neither subsection invests or confers "jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters". s 3(1): "State includes the
-- Northern Territory and the Australian Capital Territory"; "Territory does not include" them (so a "Territory" here is
-- an external Territory such as Norfolk Island). s 4 vests nothing in any other court: such a court is "not covered".
DECLARE `The other court` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court of another State (including the NT and the ACT) or of a Territory`
    `the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia)`
    `another court`

DECLARE `Can the other court hear my NSW matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has original and appellate jurisdiction in State matters (s 4(3))`
    `yes: the State Family Court of another State has original and appellate jurisdiction in State matters (s 4(4))`
    `no: s 4 vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (s 4(5))`
    `not covered: your matter is not a State matter, and s 4 vests only State matters`
    `not covered: s 4 vests jurisdiction only in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States`

GIVEN `the other court` IS A `The other court`
      `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the matter is criminal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can the other court hear my NSW matter`
`s 4 -- jurisdiction of the other court` `the other court` `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` `the matter is criminal` MEANS
    IF `the matter is criminal`
    THEN `no: s 4 vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (s 4(5))`
    ELSE IF NOT (`s 3 -- a State matter` `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8`)
         THEN `not covered: your matter is not a State matter, and s 4 vests only State matters`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the other court`
              WHEN `the Supreme Court of another State (including the NT and the ACT) or of a Territory` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has original and appellate jurisdiction in State matters (s 4(3))`
              WHEN `the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia)` THEN `yes: the State Family Court of another State has original and appellate jurisdiction in State matters (s 4(4))`
              WHEN `another court` THEN `not covered: s 4 vests jurisdiction only in the Supreme Courts and State Family Courts of other States`

@export Can a court outside NSW decide my NSW-law matter under the cross-vesting scheme (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 3, 4)?
GIVEN `the other court` IS A `The other court` @desc Which court outside NSW: the Supreme Court of another State (including the NT and the ACT) or of a Territory; the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia); or another court
      `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the Supreme Court of NSW have jurisdiction in your matter without relying on a law of the Commonwealth or of another State?
      `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your matter removed into the NSW Supreme Court from a lower NSW court or a tribunal by an order under s 8?
      `the matter is criminal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the matter a criminal matter?
GIVETH A `Can the other court hear my NSW matter`
DECIDE `Can a court of another State hear my NSW matter question` IS
    `s 4 -- jurisdiction of the other court` `the other court` `the NSW Supreme Court has its own jurisdiction` `the matter was removed to the Supreme Court under s 8` `the matter is criminal`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 6A -- a Commonwealth authority or officer acting under a State law`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 6A(1): the section applies to a proceeding (the federal matter proceeding) if "(a) a matter for determination in
-- the proceeding is covered by paragraph (c) or (e) of the definition of special federal matter ... (b) the matter ...
-- involves or relates to the exercise ... of functions or powers conferred on a Commonwealth authority, or an officer of
-- the Commonwealth, by an enactment (the State enactment) referred to in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the definition of
-- enactment in section 3 (1) of the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977 of the Commonwealth, and (c) the
-- matter ... arises out of, or relates to, another proceeding (the State matter proceeding) pending in any court of this
-- State-- (i) that arises, or a substantial part of which arises, under the State enactment or a corresponding enactment
-- of another State, and (ii) none of the matters for determination in which are covered by paragraph (c) or (e) ...
-- regardless of which proceeding was commenced first." Conditions joined by "if": all must hold. The paragraphs of the
-- Commonwealth definitions are borrowed: asked as facts.
GIVEN `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6A(1) -- the section applies` `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IF
        `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)`
    AND `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law`
    AND `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court`
    AND `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 5(1), (2), (6) -- transfer out of the NSW Supreme Court` -- situation: your case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 5(1): where a proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court and it appears to the Supreme Court that, having regard to
-- (A) whether it would have been incapable of being instituted in the Supreme Court and capable of being instituted in
-- the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, (B) the extent to which the matters are matters
-- under a law of the Commonwealth not otherwise within the Supreme Court's jurisdiction, and (C) the interests of
-- justice, "it is more appropriate that the relevant proceeding be determined by the Federal Court or the Federal
-- Circuit and Family Court of Australia, as the case may be, ... the Supreme Court shall transfer" it. A "where"
-- condition: when the court has not formed that view, the answer is no (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)).
-- s 6A(3): if the federal matter proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court and the State matter proceeding is pending in
-- any court of this State, "neither section 5 (1) nor section 6 applies to require the Supreme Court to transfer ...
-- However, the Supreme Court may do so if it considers that to be appropriate".
DECLARE `The Supreme Court's view under s 5(1)` IS ONE OF
    `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it`
    `more appropriate that the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia decide it`
    `neither`

DECLARE `Must my case go to a federal court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (s 5(1))`
    `no: s 6A(3) -- s 5(1) does not require the transfer, though the Supreme Court may transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
    `no: the Supreme Court has not formed the view s 5(1) requires, so s 5(1) does not require a transfer`

GIVEN `the court's view` IS A `The Supreme Court's view under s 5(1)`
      `s 6A applies` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must my case go to a federal court`
`s 5(1) -- transfer to a federal court` `the court's view` `s 6A applies` MEANS
    IF `s 6A applies`
    THEN `no: s 6A(3) -- s 5(1) does not require the transfer, though the Supreme Court may transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the court's view`
         WHEN `more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
         WHEN `more appropriate that the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia decide it` THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your case to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (s 5(1))`
         WHEN `neither` THEN `no: the Supreme Court has not formed the view s 5(1) requires, so s 5(1) does not require a transfer`

@export My case is in the NSW Supreme Court. Must it be sent to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 5(1), 6A(3))?
GIVEN `the court's view` IS A `The Supreme Court's view under s 5(1)` @desc The Supreme Court's view, having regard to where the case could have been brought, how far it turns on Commonwealth law, and the interests of justice: more appropriate that the Federal Court decide it; more appropriate that the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia decide it; or neither
      `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your case raise a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e) of the definition in s 3(1) of the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act?
      `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that matter concern a Commonwealth authority or officer using functions or powers given by a State law of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act definition of enactment?
      `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a related case, arising wholly or substantially under that State law (or a corresponding law of another State), pending in a NSW court?
      `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that related case raise no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)?
GIVETH A `Must my case go to a federal court`
DECIDE `Must my case go to a federal court question` IS
    `s 5(1) -- transfer to a federal court` `the court's view` (`s 6A(1) -- the section applies` `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)`)

-- s 5(2): where a proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court (the first court) and it appears to the first court that
-- "(i) the relevant proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in the Supreme Court of
-- another State or of a Territory and it is more appropriate that the relevant proceeding be determined by that other
-- Supreme Court, (ii) having regard to-- (A) ... (B) ... (C) the interests of justice, it is more appropriate that the
-- relevant proceeding be determined by that other Supreme Court, or (iii) it is otherwise in the interests of justice that
-- the relevant proceeding be determined by the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory, the first court shall
-- transfer the relevant proceeding to that other Supreme Court." Three limbs joined by "or". A "where" condition: none
-- holding, the answer is no.
DECLARE `Must my case go to another Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
    `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court, as more appropriately decided there having regard to where it could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(ii))`
    `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(iii))`
    `no: the NSW Supreme Court has not formed any of the views s 5(2) requires, so s 5(2) does not require a transfer`

GIVEN `a related case is pending in the other Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it more appropriate there because of that related case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it more appropriate there having regard to the s 5(2)(b)(ii) matters` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that it be decided there` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must my case go to another Supreme Court`
`s 5(2) -- transfer to another Supreme Court` `a related case is pending in the other Supreme Court` `the court thinks it more appropriate there because of that related case` `the court thinks it more appropriate there having regard to the s 5(2)(b)(ii) matters` `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that it be decided there` MEANS
    IF `a related case is pending in the other Supreme Court` AND `the court thinks it more appropriate there because of that related case`
    THEN `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
    ELSE IF `the court thinks it more appropriate there having regard to the s 5(2)(b)(ii) matters`
         THEN `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court, as more appropriately decided there having regard to where it could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(ii))`
         ELSE IF `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that it be decided there`
              THEN `yes: the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your case to the other Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(iii))`
              ELSE `no: the NSW Supreme Court has not formed any of the views s 5(2) requires, so s 5(2) does not require a transfer`

@export My case is in the NSW Supreme Court. Must it be sent to the Supreme Court of another State or Territory (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 5(2))?
GIVEN `a related case is pending in the other Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is another case, out of which yours arises or to which it is related, pending in that other Supreme Court?
      `the court thinks it more appropriate there because of that related case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the NSW Supreme Court consider it more appropriate that your case be decided by that other Supreme Court, given the related case?
      `the court thinks it more appropriate there having regard to the s 5(2)(b)(ii) matters` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the NSW Supreme Court consider it more appropriate that the other Supreme Court decide it, having regard to whether the case could only have been brought there, how far it turns on that other State's law, and the interests of justice?
      `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that it be decided there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the NSW Supreme Court consider it otherwise in the interests of justice that the other Supreme Court decide it?
GIVETH A `Must my case go to another Supreme Court`
DECIDE `Must my case go to another Supreme Court question` IS
    `s 5(2) -- transfer to another Supreme Court` `a related case is pending in the other Supreme Court` `the court thinks it more appropriate there because of that related case` `the court thinks it more appropriate there having regard to the s 5(2)(b)(ii) matters` `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that it be decided there`

-- s 5(6): where "(a) a court (the first court) transfers a proceeding to another court under a law or laws relating to
-- cross-vesting of jurisdiction, and (b) it appears to the first court that-- (i) there is another proceeding pending
-- in the first court that arises out of, or is related to, the first-mentioned proceeding, and (ii) it is in the
-- interests of justice that the other proceeding be determined by the other court, the first court shall transfer the
-- other proceeding to the other court." All three conditions; a "where" condition: when one fails, the answer is no.
GIVEN `the related case has been transferred` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my case is pending in the court that transferred it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my case arises out of or is related to the transferred case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it in the interests of justice that mine follow` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5(6) -- the related case follows` `the related case has been transferred` `my case is pending in the court that transferred it` `my case arises out of or is related to the transferred case` `the court thinks it in the interests of justice that mine follow` IF
        `the related case has been transferred`
    AND `my case is pending in the court that transferred it`
    AND `my case arises out of or is related to the transferred case`
    AND `the court thinks it in the interests of justice that mine follow`

@export A related case was transferred to another court under the cross-vesting laws. Must my case in the same court follow it there (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 5(6))?
GIVEN `the related case has been transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a court transferred the other case to another court under a cross-vesting law?
      `my case is pending in the court that transferred it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your case pending in the court that made that transfer?
      `my case arises out of or is related to the transferred case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your case arise out of, or relate to, the transferred case?
      `the court thinks it in the interests of justice that mine follow` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that court consider it in the interests of justice that your case be decided by the court the other case went to?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my related case follow question` IF
    `s 5(6) -- the related case follows` `the related case has been transferred` `my case is pending in the court that transferred it` `my case arises out of or is related to the transferred case` `the court thinks it in the interests of justice that mine follow`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 5(7) -- who can ask for a transfer`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 5(7): "A court may transfer a proceeding under this section on the application of a party to the proceeding, of its
-- own motion or on the application of the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory." s 3(1):
-- "party, in relation to a proceeding, includes a person who intervenes in the proceeding." The list is not closed with
-- "only": a person outside it is "not covered" (fork F2).
DECLARE `My role in the case` IS ONE OF
    `a party (plaintiff, defendant or other party)`
    `a person who has intervened in the case`
    `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Can I apply for a transfer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a party to the proceeding may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7)); the court may also transfer of its own motion`
    `yes: an intervener is a party (s 3(1) "party") and may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7))`
    `yes: an Attorney-General may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7))`
    `not covered: s 5(7) names parties and Attorneys-General; the court may still transfer of its own motion`

GIVEN `my role` IS A `My role in the case`
GIVETH A `Can I apply for a transfer`
`s 5(7) -- who may apply` `my role` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my role`
    WHEN `a party (plaintiff, defendant or other party)` THEN `yes: a party to the proceeding may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7)); the court may also transfer of its own motion`
    WHEN `a person who has intervened in the case` THEN `yes: an intervener is a party (s 3(1) "party") and may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7))`
    WHEN `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory` THEN `yes: an Attorney-General may apply for a transfer under s 5 (s 5(7))`
    WHEN `someone else` THEN `not covered: s 5(7) names parties and Attorneys-General; the court may still transfer of its own motion`

@export Can I ask the court to transfer the case to another court under the cross-vesting scheme (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 3(1), 5(7))?
GIVEN `my role` IS A `My role in the case` @desc Your role: a party (plaintiff, defendant or other party); a person who has intervened in the case; the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory; or someone else
GIVETH A `Can I apply for a transfer`
DECIDE `Can I apply for a transfer question` IS
    `s 5(7) -- who may apply` `my role`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 6 -- special federal matters` -- situation: your case in the NSW Supreme Court raises a special federal matter
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 6(1): "If-- (a) a matter for determination in a proceeding that is pending in the Supreme Court is a special federal
-- matter, and (b) the court does not make an order under subsection (3) ..., the court must transfer the proceeding ...
-- to the Federal Court or a court mentioned in subsection (2) (b)." (1A): "However, the court must only transfer so much
-- of the proceeding as is, in the opinion of the court, within the jurisdiction (including the accrued jurisdiction) of"
-- that court. (2): (a) a matter in paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition -- to the Federal
-- Court; (b) a matter in paragraph (ab) -- "to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the
-- Supreme Court of the Northern Territory, in the opinion of the court, is appropriate". (9): s 6 "does not apply to a
-- proceeding by way of an appeal that is instituted in the Full Court of the Supreme Court if the court the decision of
-- which is the subject of the appeal had made an order under subsection (3)" (or the pre-1992 s 6(1)). s 6A(3): see
-- above. The paragraphs of the Commonwealth definition are borrowed (that Act is not deposited): asked as a fact,
-- once. Paragraphs (c) and (e) are split out because s 6A(1)(a) turns on them; the s 6A(1)(a) fact is read from the
-- paragraph, not asked again, so the two cannot disagree.
DECLARE `The special federal matter paragraph` IS ONE OF
    `paragraph (a), (b) or (d) of the Commonwealth definition`
    `paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition`
    `paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition`
    `no special federal matter`

DECLARE `Must my special federal matter go elsewhere` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer to the Federal Court only the part within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (2)(b))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer only the part within the jurisdiction of whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
    `no: the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(1)(b))`
    `no: s 6 does not apply to an appeal to the Full Court where the trial court made a s 6(3) order on this matter (s 6(9))`
    `no: s 6A(3) -- s 6 does not require the transfer, though the Supreme Court may transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
    `no: your case raises no special federal matter, so s 6 does not require a transfer (s 6(1)(a))`

GIVEN `the paragraph` IS A `The special federal matter paragraph`
      `it is an appeal where the trial court kept the matter under s 6(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 6A applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the whole case is within the other court's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must my special federal matter go elsewhere`
`s 6 -- transfer of a special federal matter` `the paragraph` `it is an appeal where the trial court kept the matter under s 6(3)` `s 6A applies` `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the case` `the whole case is within the other court's jurisdiction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the paragraph`
    WHEN `no special federal matter` THEN `no: your case raises no special federal matter, so s 6 does not require a transfer (s 6(1)(a))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `it is an appeal where the trial court kept the matter under s 6(3)`
        THEN `no: s 6 does not apply to an appeal to the Full Court where the trial court made a s 6(3) order on this matter (s 6(9))`
        ELSE IF `s 6A applies`
             THEN `no: s 6A(3) -- s 6 does not require the transfer, though the Supreme Court may transfer it if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice`
             ELSE IF `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the case`
                  THEN `no: the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the proceeding itself (s 6(1)(b))`
                  ELSE CONSIDER `the paragraph`
                       WHEN `paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition` THEN
                            IF `the whole case is within the other court's jurisdiction`
                            THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (2)(b))`
                            ELSE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer only the part within the jurisdiction of whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(b))`
                       OTHERWISE
                            IF `the whole case is within the other court's jurisdiction`
                            THEN `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer the whole proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`
                            ELSE `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer to the Federal Court only the part within the Federal Court's jurisdiction, including its accrued jurisdiction (s 6(1), (1A), (2)(a))`

@export My case in the NSW Supreme Court raises a special federal matter. Must it be transferred, and to which court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 6(1), (1A), (2), (9), 6A(3))?
GIVEN `the paragraph` IS A `The special federal matter paragraph` @desc Which paragraph of the definition of special federal matter in s 3(1) of the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act your matter falls under: paragraph (a), (b) or (d); paragraph (c) or (e); paragraph (ab); or no special federal matter
      `it is an appeal where the trial court kept the matter under s 6(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is this an appeal to the Full Court of the Supreme Court from a court that had ordered under s 6(3) (or the pre-1992 s 6(1)) that it decide this special federal matter itself?
      `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Supreme Court ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the case itself?
      `the whole case is within the other court's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In the Supreme Court's opinion, is the whole of your case within the jurisdiction (including accrued jurisdiction) of the court it would go to?
      `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it concern a Commonwealth authority or officer using functions or powers given by a State law of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act definition of enactment?
      `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a related case, arising wholly or substantially under that State law (or a corresponding law of another State), pending in a NSW court?
      `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that related case raise no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)?
GIVETH A `Must my special federal matter go elsewhere`
DECIDE `Must my special federal matter be transferred question` IS
    `s 6 -- transfer of a special federal matter` `the paragraph` `it is an appeal where the trial court kept the matter under s 6(3)` (`s 6A(1) -- the section applies` (`the paragraph` EQUALS `paragraph (c) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition`) `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)`) `the Supreme Court has ordered under s 6(3) that it decide the case` `the whole case is within the other court's jurisdiction`

-- s 6(3): "The Supreme Court may order that the proceeding be determined by that court if it is satisfied that there are
-- special reasons for doing so in the particular circumstances of the proceeding other than reasons relevant to the
-- convenience of the parties." (4): "Before making an order under subsection (3), the court must be satisfied that-- (a)
-- a written notice specifying the nature of the special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of the
-- Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the State, and (b) a reasonable time has elapsed since the giving of the
-- notice for the Attorneys-General to consider whether submissions to the court should be made". "if" and "Before ...
-- must be satisfied": each failing condition is a no. The court's satisfaction is an input. s 6(6) (what the court
-- must weigh in deciding special reasons: the general rule that special federal matters go to the federal courts, and
-- any Attorney-General's submission) shapes that judgment only, so it sits in the input's gloss.
DECLARE `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it decide the case itself (s 6(3), (4))`
    `no: the Supreme Court cannot keep it until written notice of the special federal matter has been given to both the Commonwealth and the NSW Attorneys-General (s 6(4)(a))`
    `no: the Supreme Court cannot keep it until a reasonable time has passed for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b))`
    `no: the Supreme Court may keep it only for special reasons other than the parties' convenience (s 6(3))`

GIVEN `notice has been given to both Attorneys-General` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has passed since the notice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied there are special reasons other than convenience` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter`
`s 6(3), (4) -- the Supreme Court keeping the case` `notice has been given to both Attorneys-General` `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has passed since the notice` `the court is satisfied there are special reasons other than convenience` MEANS
    IF (NOT `notice has been given to both Attorneys-General`)
    THEN `no: the Supreme Court cannot keep it until written notice of the special federal matter has been given to both the Commonwealth and the NSW Attorneys-General (s 6(4)(a))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has passed since the notice`)
         THEN `no: the Supreme Court cannot keep it until a reasonable time has passed for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions (s 6(4)(b))`
         ELSE IF `the court is satisfied there are special reasons other than convenience`
              THEN `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it decide the case itself (s 6(3), (4))`
              ELSE `no: the Supreme Court may keep it only for special reasons other than the parties' convenience (s 6(3))`

@export My case raises a special federal matter. Can the NSW Supreme Court keep it and decide it itself (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 6(3), (4))?
GIVEN `notice has been given to both Attorneys-General` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has written notice of the nature of the special federal matter been given to both the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of NSW?
      `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has passed since the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that a reasonable time has passed since the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions?
      `the court is satisfied there are special reasons other than convenience` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied there are special reasons, in the particular circumstances, other than the parties' convenience, for it to decide the case? (In deciding this the court must weigh the general rule that special federal matters go to the Federal Court or the court in s 6(2)(b), and any submission by an Attorney-General: s 6(6).)
GIVETH A `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter`
DECIDE `Can the Supreme Court keep my special federal matter question` IS
    `s 6(3), (4) -- the Supreme Court keeping the case` `notice has been given to both Attorneys-General` `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has passed since the notice` `the court is satisfied there are special reasons other than convenience`

-- s 6(5): "For the purposes of subsection (4), the court-- ... (b) may direct a party to the proceeding to give a notice
-- in accordance with that subsection." The Act fixes no period: the direction does. Encoded as a regulative rule on the
-- directed party, with the period of the direction (in days) as WITHIN. Failing to comply is a breach of the direction,
-- and leaves s 6(4)(a) unmet (so the court cannot keep the case under s 6(3)).
DECLARE `Cross-vesting party` IS ONE OF
    `the party directed to give notice`

DECLARE `Cross-vesting act` IS ONE OF
    `give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General`

GIVEN `the days the direction allows` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Cross-vesting party` `Cross-vesting act`
`s 6(5)(b) -- the directed notice to the Attorneys-General` `the days the direction allows` MEANS
    PARTY `the party directed to give notice`
    MUST `give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General`
    WITHIN `the days the direction allows`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the party directed to give notice` BECAUSE "s 6(5)(b): the party the court directed did not give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General within the time the direction allowed; until notice is given, s 6(4)(a) bars an order under s 6(3)"

-- Runs the rule over "the notice was given at this time" (days after the direction) and answers whether that is in
-- time. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH. Takes a DEONTIC value, so it is
-- never exported.
GIVEN `the time` IS A NUMBER
      `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Cross-vesting party` `Cross-vesting act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `notice given at` `the time` `is in time under` `the rule` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time`), (EVENT `the party directed to give notice` `give written notice of the special federal matter to both Attorneys-General` `the time`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Is my notice in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court's direction allowed`
    `no: you gave the notice, but after the time the court's direction allowed had ended`
    `not yet due: you have not given the notice, but the time the direction allows has not ended`
    `overdue: the time the direction allows has ended and you have not given the notice; until it is given the court cannot keep the case under s 6(3)`

GIVEN `the day of the direction` IS A DATE
      `the days the direction allows` IS A NUMBER
      `I have given the notice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I gave the notice` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Is my notice in time`
`s 6(5)(b) -- the state of the directed notice` `the day of the direction` `the days the direction allows` `I have given the notice` `the day I gave the notice` `today` MEANS
    IF `I have given the notice`
    THEN IF `notice given at` (DATE_SERIAL `the day I gave the notice` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day of the direction`) `is in time under` (`s 6(5)(b) -- the directed notice to the Attorneys-General` `the days the direction allows`)
         THEN `yes: you gave the notice within the time the court's direction allowed`
         ELSE `no: you gave the notice, but after the time the court's direction allowed had ended`
    ELSE IF `notice given at` (DATE_SERIAL `today` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day of the direction`) `is in time under` (`s 6(5)(b) -- the directed notice to the Attorneys-General` `the days the direction allows`)
         THEN `not yet due: you have not given the notice, but the time the direction allows has not ended`
         ELSE `overdue: the time the direction allows has ended and you have not given the notice; until it is given the court cannot keep the case under s 6(3)`

@export The court directed me to give the Attorneys-General notice of the special federal matter. Have I done it in time (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 6(4), (5)(b))?
GIVEN `the day of the direction` IS A DATE @desc The date the court gave the direction (ISO 8601)
      `the days the direction allows` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the direction the court allowed for giving the notice
      `I have given the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you given written notice of the special federal matter to both the Commonwealth and the NSW Attorneys-General?
      `the day I gave the notice` IS A DATE @desc The date you gave it, to the second of them if on different days (ISO 8601); if not yet, any date
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Is my notice in time`
DECIDE `Is my notice to the Attorneys-General in time question` IS
    `s 6(5)(b) -- the state of the directed notice` `the day of the direction` `the days the direction allows` `I have given the notice` `the day I gave the notice` `today`

-- s 6(7): "Nothing in this section prevents the court granting urgent relief of an interlocutory nature if it is in the
-- interests of justice to do so." A saving: it permits, it does not entitle. Outside it, s 6 is silent ("not covered").
DECLARE `Can I still get urgent interim relief` IS ONE OF
    `yes: s 6 does not stop the Supreme Court granting you urgent interlocutory relief, if it is in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
    `not covered: s 6(7) saves only urgent interlocutory relief that the court considers in the interests of justice`

GIVEN `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I still get urgent interim relief`
`s 6(7) -- urgent interlocutory relief` `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` `the court thinks it in the interests of justice` MEANS
    IF `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` AND `the court thinks it in the interests of justice`
    THEN `yes: s 6 does not stop the Supreme Court granting you urgent interlocutory relief, if it is in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
    ELSE `not covered: s 6(7) saves only urgent interlocutory relief that the court considers in the interests of justice`

@export My case raises a special federal matter. Can the NSW Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief before any transfer (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 6(7))?
GIVEN `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the relief you seek urgent and interlocutory (interim, such as an injunction until trial)?
      `the court thinks it in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court consider granting it to be in the interests of justice?
GIVETH A `Can I still get urgent interim relief`
DECIDE `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` IS
    `s 6(7) -- urgent interlocutory relief` `the relief is urgent and interlocutory` `the court thinks it in the interests of justice`

-- s 6(8): if, "through inadvertence", the Supreme Court determines a s 6(1) proceeding without a s 6(3) order or the s
-- 6(4) notice, "nothing in this section invalidates the decision". s 7(7): where the Full Court "(a) determines a
-- proceeding to which subsection (4) applies as mentioned in subsection (6), or (b) through inadvertence, determines a
-- proceeding to which subsection (4) applies, nothing in this section invalidates the decision of that Court." Outside
-- those limbs the sections are silent: "not covered".
DECLARE `What went wrong` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court decided my special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice`
    `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court, choosing to proceed in the interests of justice under s 7(6)`
    `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court`

DECLARE `Does the irregularity invalidate the decision` IS ONE OF
    `no: s 6 does not invalidate a decision made through inadvertence (s 6(8))`
    `no: s 7 does not invalidate the Full Court's decision (s 7(7)(a))`
    `no: s 7 does not invalidate a decision the Full Court made through inadvertence (s 7(7)(b))`
    `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only the decisions they describe; the Act does not say what follows otherwise`

GIVEN `what went wrong` IS A `What went wrong`
      `it was through inadvertence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Does the irregularity invalidate the decision`
`ss 6(8), 7(7) -- decisions saved` `what went wrong` `it was through inadvertence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what went wrong`
    WHEN `the Supreme Court decided my special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice` THEN
         IF `it was through inadvertence`
         THEN `no: s 6 does not invalidate a decision made through inadvertence (s 6(8))`
         ELSE `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only the decisions they describe; the Act does not say what follows otherwise`
    WHEN `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court, choosing to proceed in the interests of justice under s 7(6)` THEN `no: s 7 does not invalidate the Full Court's decision (s 7(7)(a))`
    WHEN `the Full Court decided my appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court` THEN
         IF `it was through inadvertence`
         THEN `no: s 7 does not invalidate a decision the Full Court made through inadvertence (s 7(7)(b))`
         ELSE `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only the decisions they describe; the Act does not say what follows otherwise`

@export The NSW Supreme Court decided my case when it should have gone to a federal court. Is the decision invalid for that reason (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 6(8), 7(7))?
GIVEN `what went wrong` IS A `What went wrong` @desc What happened: the Supreme Court decided your special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice; the Full Court decided your appeal that should have gone to a federal Full Court, choosing to proceed in the interests of justice under s 7(6); or the Full Court decided such an appeal otherwise
      `it was through inadvertence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did it happen through inadvertence (an oversight)?
GIVETH A `Does the irregularity invalidate the decision`
DECIDE `Is the decision invalid question` IS
    `ss 6(8), 7(7) -- decisions saved` `what went wrong` `it was through inadvertence`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 5(3), (4), 6A(2) -- transfer into the NSW Supreme Court` -- situation: your case is in another Supreme Court or a federal court
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 5(3): a proceeding pending in the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory (the first court) "shall" be
-- transferred to the Supreme Court of New South Wales where it appears to the first court that (i) it arises out of or
-- is related to another proceeding pending in the NSW Supreme Court and it is more appropriate there, (ii) having regard
-- to (A)-(C) it is more appropriate there, or (iii) it is otherwise in the interests of justice. s 5(4): the same for a
-- proceeding pending in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, with (A)-(D). s 6A(2):
-- for a s 6A federal matter proceeding pending in a federal court, the federal court "may transfer the proceeding to the
-- Supreme Court. Section 5 (4) does not apply to the federal matter proceeding." Its test is the interests of justice,
-- "including the desirability of related proceedings being heard in the same jurisdiction": a view that NSW is more
-- appropriate because of a related case pending there (limb (i)) or a view that it is otherwise in the interests of
-- justice (limb (iii)) each stands for that s 6A(2)(b) view (fork F6).
DECLARE `Where my case is` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory`
    `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia`

DECLARE `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(3)(b)(i) or (4)(b)(i))`
    `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as more appropriately decided there having regard to where it could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(ii) or (4)(b)(ii))`
    `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(iii) or (4)(b)(iii))`
    `may: s 5(4) does not apply to your case (s 6A(2)), but the federal court may transfer it to the NSW Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related cases together`
    `no: s 6A(2) -- s 5(4) does not apply, and the federal court does not consider a transfer appropriate`
    `no: the court has not formed any of the views s 5(3) or (4) requires, so it is not required to transfer your case`

GIVEN `where my case is` IS A `Where my case is`
      `s 6A applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a related case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW because of that related case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW having regard to the listed matters` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that NSW decide it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court`
`ss 5(3), (4), 6A(2) -- transfer into the NSW Supreme Court` `where my case is` `s 6A applies` `a related case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court` `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW because of that related case` `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW having regard to the listed matters` `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that NSW decide it` MEANS
    IF `s 6A applies` AND `where my case is` EQUALS `the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia`
    THEN IF (`a related case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court` AND `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW because of that related case`)
            OR `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that NSW decide it`
         THEN `may: s 5(4) does not apply to your case (s 6A(2)), but the federal court may transfer it to the NSW Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice, including keeping related cases together`
         ELSE `no: s 6A(2) -- s 5(4) does not apply, and the federal court does not consider a transfer appropriate`
    ELSE IF `a related case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court` AND `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW because of that related case`
         THEN `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as it is related to a case pending there and more appropriately decided there (s 5(3)(b)(i) or (4)(b)(i))`
         ELSE IF `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW having regard to the listed matters`
              THEN `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court, as more appropriately decided there having regard to where it could have been brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(ii) or (4)(b)(ii))`
              ELSE IF `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that NSW decide it`
                   THEN `yes: the court must transfer your case to the NSW Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(iii) or (4)(b)(iii))`
                   ELSE `no: the court has not formed any of the views s 5(3) or (4) requires, so it is not required to transfer your case`

@export My case is in another State's Supreme Court or a federal court. Must it be sent to the NSW Supreme Court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 5(3), (4), 6A(2))?
GIVEN `where my case is` IS A `Where my case is` @desc Where your case is now: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory; or the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia
      `a related case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is another case, out of which yours arises or to which it is related, pending in the NSW Supreme Court?
      `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW because of that related case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court your case is in consider it more appropriate that the NSW Supreme Court decide it, given the related case?
      `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW having regard to the listed matters` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that court consider it more appropriate that the NSW Supreme Court decide it, having regard to where the case could have been brought, how far it turns on NSW law, and the interests of justice?
      `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that NSW decide it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that court consider it otherwise in the interests of justice (including keeping related cases together) that the NSW Supreme Court decide it?
      `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your case raise a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e) of the definition in s 3(1) of the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act?
      `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that matter concern a Commonwealth authority or officer using functions or powers given by a State law of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act definition of enactment?
      `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a related case, arising wholly or substantially under that State law (or a corresponding law of another State), pending in a NSW court?
      `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that related case raise no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)?
GIVETH A `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court`
DECIDE `Must my case come to the NSW Supreme Court question` IS
    `ss 5(3), (4), 6A(2) -- transfer into the NSW Supreme Court` `where my case is` (`s 6A(1) -- the section applies` `my case raises a special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)` `it concerns a Commonwealth authority or officer using powers under a State law` `a related case under that State law is pending in a NSW court` `the related case raises no special federal matter under paragraph (c) or (e)`) `a related case is pending in the NSW Supreme Court` `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW because of that related case` `the court thinks it more appropriate in NSW having regard to the listed matters` `the court thinks it otherwise in the interests of justice that NSW decide it`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 8, 10 -- moving a case between the NSW Supreme Court and lower courts or tribunals` -- situation: your case is in a lower NSW court or tribunal, or a consumer case in a Supreme Court
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 8(1): where a proceeding is pending in "(i) a court, other than the Supreme Court, of the State, or (ii) a tribunal
-- established by or under an Act" and it appears to the Supreme Court that "(i) the relevant proceeding arises out of, or
-- is related to, another proceeding pending in the Federal Court, the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia or
-- the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory and, if an order is made ..., there would be grounds on which that
-- other proceeding could be transferred to the Supreme Court, or (ii) an order should be made ... so that consideration
-- can be given to whether the relevant proceeding should be transferred to another court, the Supreme Court may, on the
-- application of a party to the relevant proceeding or of its own motion, make an order removing the relevant proceeding
-- to the Supreme Court." (2): the Act then applies as if it were pending in the Supreme Court. (3): the Supreme Court may
-- remit it "if the Supreme Court considers it appropriate". A "where" condition: no limb, the answer is no.
DECLARE `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court may, on your application or its own motion, remove your case into the Supreme Court, so that the related case could be brought to it (s 8(1)(b)(i)); it may later send it back (s 8(3))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court may, on your application or its own motion, remove your case into the Supreme Court so that a transfer to another court can be considered (s 8(1)(b)(ii)); it may later send it back (s 8(3))`
    `no: s 8 reaches only cases pending in a NSW court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act`
    `no: the Supreme Court has not formed either view s 8(1)(b) requires`

DECLARE `Where my case is pending` IS ONE OF
    `a NSW court other than the Supreme Court`
    `a tribunal established by or under an Act`
    `somewhere else`

GIVEN `where it is pending` IS A `Where my case is pending`
      `a related case is pending in a federal court or another Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court thinks the related case could then be transferred to it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court`
`s 8 -- removal into the Supreme Court` `where it is pending` `a related case is pending in a federal court or another Supreme Court` `the Supreme Court thinks the related case could then be transferred to it` `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer` MEANS
    IF `where it is pending` EQUALS `somewhere else`
    THEN `no: s 8 reaches only cases pending in a NSW court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act`
    ELSE IF `a related case is pending in a federal court or another Supreme Court` AND `the Supreme Court thinks the related case could then be transferred to it`
         THEN `yes: the Supreme Court may, on your application or its own motion, remove your case into the Supreme Court, so that the related case could be brought to it (s 8(1)(b)(i)); it may later send it back (s 8(3))`
         ELSE IF `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer`
              THEN `yes: the Supreme Court may, on your application or its own motion, remove your case into the Supreme Court so that a transfer to another court can be considered (s 8(1)(b)(ii)); it may later send it back (s 8(3))`
              ELSE `no: the Supreme Court has not formed either view s 8(1)(b) requires`

@export My case is in a lower NSW court or a tribunal. Can the NSW Supreme Court take it over under the cross-vesting scheme (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 8)?
GIVEN `where it is pending` IS A `Where my case is pending` @desc Where your case is pending: a NSW court other than the Supreme Court; a tribunal established by or under an Act; or somewhere else
      `a related case is pending in a federal court or another Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is another case, out of which yours arises or to which it is related, pending in the Federal Court, the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, or the Supreme Court of another State or Territory?
      `the Supreme Court thinks the related case could then be transferred to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Supreme Court consider that, if your case were removed, there would be grounds to transfer that other case to the Supreme Court?
      `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Supreme Court consider your case should be removed so that it can consider transferring it to another court?
GIVETH A `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court`
DECIDE `Can my case be removed into the Supreme Court question` IS
    `s 8 -- removal into the Supreme Court` `where it is pending` `a related case is pending in a federal court or another Supreme Court` `the Supreme Court thinks the related case could then be transferred to it` `the Supreme Court thinks removal is needed to consider a transfer`

-- s 10: where "(a) a proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court or the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory,
-- (b) a matter for determination in the proceeding is a matter arising under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade
-- Practices Act 1974 of the Commonwealth, (c) no matter for determination in the proceeding is a special federal matter,
-- (d) the proceeding is not a proceeding by way of an appeal from a judgment of a court, and (e) a court of the State,
-- other than the Supreme Court, has jurisdiction with respect to all of the matters for determination in the
-- proceeding, the court referred to in paragraph (a) may, on the application of a party to the proceeding or of its own
-- motion, transfer the proceeding to the court referred to in paragraph (e)." All five; a "where" condition: when one
-- fails, the answer is no. (Finding 4: the Trade Practices Act provisions named have since been replaced.)
GIVEN `it is pending in a Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it raises a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it raises a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a lower NSW court has jurisdiction over every matter in it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10 -- transfer down of a consumer protection case` `it is pending in a Supreme Court` `it raises a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974` `it raises a special federal matter` `it is an appeal` `a lower NSW court has jurisdiction over every matter in it` IF
        `it is pending in a Supreme Court`
    AND `it raises a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974`
    AND (NOT `it raises a special federal matter`)
    AND (NOT `it is an appeal`)
    AND `a lower NSW court has jurisdiction over every matter in it`

@export My consumer protection case under the Trade Practices Act is in a Supreme Court. Can it be sent down to a lower NSW court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 10)?
GIVEN `it is pending in a Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your case pending in the NSW Supreme Court or the Supreme Court of another State or Territory?
      `it raises a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it raise a matter arising under Division 1 or 1A of Part V (consumer protection, product safety and information) of the Trade Practices Act 1974 (Cth)?
      `it raises a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does any matter in it fall within the definition of special federal matter in the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act?
      `it is an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it an appeal from a judgment of a court?
      `a lower NSW court has jurisdiction over every matter in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a NSW court other than the Supreme Court (such as the District Court or the Local Court) have jurisdiction over all the matters in it?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my consumer case be sent to a lower court question` IF
    `s 10 -- transfer down of a consumer protection case` `it is pending in a Supreme Court` `it raises a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974` `it raises a special federal matter` `it is an appeal` `a lower NSW court has jurisdiction over every matter in it`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 7 -- appeals` -- situation: you want to appeal a decision
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 7(1): "An appeal shall not be instituted from a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal
-- Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the Full Court of the Supreme Court." (2): "An appeal shall not be instituted
-- from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the other of those courts." (3): where
-- the only matters in an appeal from a single judge of the Supreme Court are matters other than matters arising under an
-- Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act, the appeal "shall be instituted only in, and shall be
-- determined only by, the Full Court of the Supreme Court." (4): where a matter in such an appeal (not one under (5))
-- arises under a Schedule Act, it "shall be instituted only in, and shall be determined only by-- (a) the Full Court of
-- the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, as the case requires, or (b) with special
-- leave of the High Court, the High Court." (5): an appeal from a judge of a State Family Court involving both a Schedule
-- matter and another matter "may be dealt with as if no matter ... were" a Schedule matter. Flat negatives and "only"
-- lists: the answer is no outside them. The Schedule (in the Commonwealth Act, not deposited) is asked as a fact.
-- s 7(5) says only how the appeal "may be dealt with", never where it goes, so its answer does not depend on the
-- court asked about and is never a yes for that court (finding 5).
-- Appeals from other decisions are outside s 7: "not covered".
DECLARE `The decision I want to appeal` IS ONE OF
    `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court`
    `a decision of a judge of a State Family Court`
    `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia`
    `a decision of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (any other)`
    `another decision`

DECLARE `The court I want to appeal to` IS ONE OF
    `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)`
    `the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia`
    `the High Court, with special leave`
    `the other of the two federal courts`

DECLARE `Can I bring my appeal there` IS ONE OF
    `yes: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act goes only to the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
    `yes: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act goes only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, as the case requires (s 7(4)(a))`
    `yes, with special leave: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act may go to the High Court with its special leave (s 7(4)(b))`
    `no: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act may be brought only in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
    `no: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act may be brought only in a federal Full Court or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(4))`
    `no: an appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia cannot be brought in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(1))`
    `no: an appeal cannot be brought from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the other of those courts (s 7(2))`
    `not decided: s 7(5) lets this State Family Court appeal be dealt with as if it raised no Schedule matter, but does not say which court hears it, and NSW has no State Family Court (s 3(2)), so the Act names no court for it`
    `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`

GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision I want to appeal`
      `the court` IS A `The court I want to appeal to`
      `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is another matter as well` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I bring my appeal there`
`s 7 -- where an appeal goes` `the decision` `the court` `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` `there is another matter as well` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `a decision of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court` THEN
         IF `a matter arises under a Schedule Act`
         THEN CONSIDER `the court`
              WHEN `the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` THEN `yes: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act goes only to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia, as the case requires (s 7(4)(a))`
              WHEN `the High Court, with special leave` THEN `yes, with special leave: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act may go to the High Court with its special leave (s 7(4)(b))`
              OTHERWISE `no: an appeal raising a matter under a Schedule Act may be brought only in a federal Full Court or, with special leave, the High Court (s 7(4))`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the court`
              WHEN `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` THEN `yes: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act goes only to the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
              OTHERWISE `no: an appeal with no matter arising under a Schedule Act may be brought only in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(3))`
    WHEN `a decision of a judge of a State Family Court` THEN
         IF `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` AND `there is another matter as well`
         THEN `not decided: s 7(5) lets this State Family Court appeal be dealt with as if it raised no Schedule matter, but does not say which court hears it, and NSW has no State Family Court (s 3(2)), so the Act names no court for it`
         ELSE `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`
    WHEN `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia` THEN
         CONSIDER `the court`
         WHEN `the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal)` THEN `no: an appeal from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia cannot be brought in the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (s 7(1))`
         WHEN `the other of the two federal courts` THEN `no: an appeal cannot be brought from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the other of those courts (s 7(2))`
         OTHERWISE `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`
    WHEN `a decision of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (any other)` THEN
         CONSIDER `the court`
         WHEN `the other of the two federal courts` THEN `no: an appeal cannot be brought from the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia to the other of those courts (s 7(2))`
         OTHERWISE `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`
    WHEN `another decision` THEN `not covered: s 7 does not decide where this appeal goes`

@export I want to appeal. Can I bring my appeal in the court I have in mind (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 7(1)-(5))?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision I want to appeal` @desc The decision you want to appeal: of a single judge of the NSW Supreme Court; of a judge of a State Family Court; of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia; another decision of one of those federal courts; or another decision
      `the court` IS A `The court I want to appeal to` @desc The court you want to appeal to: the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal); the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia; the High Court with special leave; or the other of the two federal courts
      `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does any matter in the appeal arise under an Act listed in the Schedule to the Commonwealth Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987?
      `there is another matter as well` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the appeal also involve another matter, not arising under a Schedule Act?
GIVETH A `Can I bring my appeal there`
DECIDE `Can I bring my appeal in that court question` IS
    `s 7 -- where an appeal goes` `the decision` `the court` `a matter arises under a Schedule Act` `there is another matter as well`

-- s 7(6): where "(a) the Full Court of the Supreme Court commences to hear a proceeding by way of an appeal, and (b)
-- before the Court determines the proceeding, it appears to the Court that the proceeding is a proceeding to which
-- subsection (4) applies, the Court shall, unless the interests of justice require that the Court proceed to determine
-- the proceeding, transfer the proceeding to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family
-- Court of Australia, as the case requires." "Unless" once met: the duty to transfer falls away (fork F3).
DECLARE `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (s 7(6))`
    `no: the interests of justice require the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court to go on and decide your appeal (s 7(6)); its decision stands (s 7(7)(a))`
    `no: s 7(6) applies only once the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court has begun hearing an appeal that raises a matter under a Schedule Act`

GIVEN `the Court of Appeal has begun hearing my appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it appears the appeal raises a matter under a Schedule Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court`
`s 7(6) -- transfer of an appeal begun in the Court of Appeal` `the Court of Appeal has begun hearing my appeal` `it appears the appeal raises a matter under a Schedule Act` `the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on` MEANS
    IF `the Court of Appeal has begun hearing my appeal` AND `it appears the appeal raises a matter under a Schedule Act`
    THEN IF `the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on`
         THEN `no: the interests of justice require the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court to go on and decide your appeal (s 7(6)); its decision stands (s 7(7)(a))`
         ELSE `yes: the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court must transfer your appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (s 7(6))`
    ELSE `no: s 7(6) applies only once the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court has begun hearing an appeal that raises a matter under a Schedule Act`

@export My appeal is being heard by the NSW Court of Appeal and turns out to raise a matter under a Schedule Act. Must it move to a federal Full Court (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 7(6), (7))?
GIVEN `the Court of Appeal has begun hearing my appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Full Court of the NSW Supreme Court (the Court of Appeal) begun hearing your appeal?
      `it appears the appeal raises a matter under a Schedule Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before deciding it, has it appeared to that Court that the appeal raises a matter arising under an Act listed in the Schedule to the Commonwealth cross-vesting Act (an appeal under s 7(4))?
      `the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that Court consider the interests of justice require it to go on and decide the appeal?
GIVETH A `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court`
DECIDE `Must my appeal move to a federal Full Court question` IS
    `s 7(6) -- transfer of an appeal begun in the Court of Appeal` `the Court of Appeal has begun hearing my appeal` `it appears the appeal raises a matter under a Schedule Act` `the interests of justice require the Court of Appeal to go on`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 5(8), 11, 12, 13, 14 -- after a transfer` -- situation: your case has been transferred or removed
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 11(1): where a court will or is likely to be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction in determining a matter "(a)
-- subject to paragraphs (b) and (c), the court shall ... apply the law in force in the State or Territory in which the
-- court is sitting (including choice of law rules), (b) subject to paragraph (c), if that matter is a right of action
-- arising under a written law of another State or Territory, the court shall ... apply the written and unwritten law of
-- that other State or Territory, and (c) the rules of evidence and procedure ... shall be such as the court considers
-- appropriate in the circumstances, being rules that are applied in a superior court in Australia or in an external
-- Territory." A "where" condition: when the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction, s 11 says nothing
-- ("not covered", the court applies its ordinary law; fork F4).
DECLARE `Which law applies to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the law of the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules used in a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules used in a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `not covered: s 11 applies only where the court is, or is likely to be, exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`

GIVEN `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Which law applies to my claim`
`s 11(1) -- the law to be applied` `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction` `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`)
    THEN `not covered: s 11 applies only where the court is, or is likely to be, exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`
    ELSE IF `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory`
         THEN `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules used in a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
         ELSE `the law of the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from rules used in a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

@export Which law will the court apply to my claim when it uses cross-vested jurisdiction (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 11(1))?
GIVEN `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the court, or is it likely to, decide your matter using jurisdiction given by a cross-vesting law (for example after a transfer)?
      `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your right of action (your claim) arise under a statute of a State or Territory other than the one where the court sits?
GIVETH A `Which law applies to my claim`
DECIDE `Which law applies to my claim question` IS
    `s 11(1) -- the law to be applied` `the court is exercising cross-vested jurisdiction` `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory`

-- s 11(3): where a proceeding is transferred or removed, "the transferee court shall deal with the proceeding as if,
-- subject to any order of the transferee court, the steps that had been taken for the purposes of the proceeding in the
-- transferor court (including the making of an order), or similar steps, had been taken in the transferee court."
-- s 12: "Where a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court, that court may make an order as to costs that relate to
-- the conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal if those costs have not already been dealt with by
-- another court." "if": already dealt with, the answer is no.
DECLARE `What carries over after the transfer` IS ONE OF
    `the steps already taken, and orders made, in the old court count as taken in the new court, unless the new court orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the new court may order costs of the earlier stage, as they have not been dealt with (s 12)`
    `the steps already taken, and orders made, in the old court count as taken in the new court, unless the new court orders otherwise (s 11(3)); s 12 gives no power over costs of the earlier stage already dealt with by another court`
    `not covered: ss 11(3) and 12 apply only once a case has been transferred or removed`

GIVEN `my case has been transferred or removed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the earlier costs have already been dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What carries over after the transfer`
`ss 11(3), 12 -- steps and costs carried over` `my case has been transferred or removed` `the earlier costs have already been dealt with` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my case has been transferred or removed`)
    THEN `not covered: ss 11(3) and 12 apply only once a case has been transferred or removed`
    ELSE IF `the earlier costs have already been dealt with`
         THEN `the steps already taken, and orders made, in the old court count as taken in the new court, unless the new court orders otherwise (s 11(3)); s 12 gives no power over costs of the earlier stage already dealt with by another court`
         ELSE `the steps already taken, and orders made, in the old court count as taken in the new court, unless the new court orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the new court may order costs of the earlier stage, as they have not been dealt with (s 12)`

@export My case was transferred. Do the steps already taken still count, and can the new court deal with the costs of the earlier stage (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) ss 11(3), 12)?
GIVEN `my case has been transferred or removed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your case been transferred or removed to another court?
      `the earlier costs have already been dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the costs of the case before the transfer already been dealt with by another court?
GIVETH A `What carries over after the transfer`
DECIDE `What carries over after the transfer question` IS
    `ss 11(3), 12 -- steps and costs carried over` `my case has been transferred or removed` `the earlier costs have already been dealt with`

-- s 13: "An appeal does not lie from a decision of a court-- (a) in relation to the transfer or removal of a proceeding
-- under this Act, or (b) as to which rules of evidence and procedure are to be applied pursuant to section 11 (1)." A flat
-- negative: no. For any other decision s 13 is silent ("not covered").
DECLARE `The decision I want to challenge` IS ONE OF
    `a decision about transferring or removing the case under this Act`
    `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)`
    `another decision`

DECLARE `Can I appeal that decision` IS ONE OF
    `no: no appeal lies from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    `no: no appeal lies from a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b))`
    `not covered: s 13 bars appeals only from transfer and removal decisions and s 11(1) evidence and procedure decisions`

GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision I want to challenge`
GIVETH A `Can I appeal that decision`
`s 13 -- no appeal` `the decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `a decision about transferring or removing the case under this Act` THEN `no: no appeal lies from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    WHEN `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)` THEN `no: no appeal lies from a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1) (s 13(b))`
    WHEN `another decision` THEN `not covered: s 13 bars appeals only from transfer and removal decisions and s 11(1) evidence and procedure decisions`

@export Can I appeal the court's decision to transfer (or not transfer) my case, or its choice of rules of evidence and procedure (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 13)?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `The decision I want to challenge` @desc The decision: about transferring or removing the case under this Act; on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1); or another decision
GIVETH A `Can I appeal that decision`
DECIDE `Can I appeal the transfer decision question` IS
    `s 13 -- no appeal` `the decision`

-- s 5(8): "A person who is entitled to practise as an Australian legal practitioner in a court has, if a proceeding ...
-- in that court is transferred to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction, the same
-- entitlement to practise in relation to-- (a) the transferred proceedings, and (b) any other proceeding out of which the
-- transferred proceeding arises or to which the transferred proceeding is related, being another proceeding that is to
-- be determined together with the transferred proceeding, in the other court that the person would have if the other
-- court were a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction." Outside its "if", s 5(8) is silent ("not covered").
DECLARE `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, in your transferred case, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8)(a))`
    `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, in your related case decided together with the transferred one, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8)(b))`
    `not covered: s 5(8) gives the right only to a lawyer entitled to practise in the court the case was transferred from, for the transferred case and related cases decided with it`

GIVEN `my lawyer may practise in the old court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my case is the transferred case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my case is related and decided together with it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court`
`s 5(8) -- the lawyer's right to practise` `my lawyer may practise in the old court` `my case is the transferred case` `my case is related and decided together with it` MEANS
    IF `my lawyer may practise in the old court` AND `my case is the transferred case`
    THEN `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, in your transferred case, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8)(a))`
    ELSE IF `my lawyer may practise in the old court` AND `my case is related and decided together with it`
         THEN `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, in your related case decided together with the transferred one, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8)(b))`
         ELSE `not covered: s 5(8) gives the right only to a lawyer entitled to practise in the court the case was transferred from, for the transferred case and related cases decided with it`

@export My case was transferred to a court in another State or a federal court. Can my lawyer keep acting for me there (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 5(8))?
GIVEN `my lawyer may practise in the old court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your lawyer entitled to practise as an Australian legal practitioner in the court the case was transferred from?
      `my case is the transferred case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your case the one that was transferred?
      `my case is related and decided together with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If not, is your case one out of which the transferred case arises or to which it is related, and is it to be decided together with it?
GIVETH A `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court`
DECIDE `Can my lawyer act for me in the new court question` IS
    `s 5(8) -- the lawyer's right to practise` `my lawyer may practise in the old court` `my case is the transferred case` `my case is related and decided together with it`

-- s 14(2): "A judgment of the Supreme Court that is given, in whole or in part, in the exercise of jurisdiction conferred
-- by a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction is enforceable in the State as if the judgment had been
-- given entirely in the exercise of the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court apart from any such law." Outside its terms,
-- s 14(2) is silent: a judgment given wholly in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction needs no help from it.
DECLARE `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a NSW Supreme Court judgment given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction is enforceable in NSW as if given in the Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
    `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of NSW Supreme Court judgments given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`

GIVEN `the judgment is of the NSW Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW`
`s 14(2) -- enforcement of the judgment` `the judgment is of the NSW Supreme Court` `it was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction` MEANS
    IF `the judgment is of the NSW Supreme Court` AND `it was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`
    THEN `yes: a NSW Supreme Court judgment given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction is enforceable in NSW as if given in the Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
    ELSE `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of NSW Supreme Court judgments given wholly or partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`

@export I won a judgment in the NSW Supreme Court in a cross-vested case. Can I enforce it in NSW like any other Supreme Court judgment (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (NSW) s 14(2))?
GIVEN `the judgment is of the NSW Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judgment one of the NSW Supreme Court?
      `it was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it given wholly or partly in jurisdiction conferred by a cross-vesting law?
GIVETH A `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW`
DECIDE `Can my judgment be enforced in NSW question` IS
    `s 14(2) -- enforcement of the judgment` `the judgment is of the NSW Supreme Court` `it was given at least partly in cross-vested jurisdiction`
